1
00:00:25,859 --> 00:00:28,124
♪♪

2
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:38,305
♪♪

3
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,511
♪♪

4
00:01:16,443 --> 00:01:17,720
You can't do this
to me, Richard.

5
00:01:17,744 --> 00:01:20,356
Think of it as an
early retirement.

6
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:21,891
I'm not ready to retire.

7
00:01:21,915 --> 00:01:24,160
You're ready, A.C. Believe me.

8
00:01:24,184 --> 00:01:26,296
But the three of us started
this company together...

9
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:27,564
You, Marvin and I.

10
00:01:27,588 --> 00:01:30,266
How can you just throw
34 years out the window?!

11
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:32,602
Your brother is dead.

12
00:01:32,626 --> 00:01:34,837
And with all the good
that you've been doing

13
00:01:34,861 --> 00:01:37,373
around here lately, you
might as well be dead, too.

14
00:01:37,397 --> 00:01:42,078
I know I've had some problems...

15
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:45,782
It's happening, isn't it?

16
00:01:45,806 --> 00:01:48,351
Richard giving him the axe.

17
00:01:51,011 --> 00:01:52,589
You can't fire me!

18
00:01:52,613 --> 00:01:55,191
I won't let you just toss me
out like an old newspaper!

19
00:01:55,215 --> 00:01:56,526
A.C., take it easy.

20
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:58,261
I'll sue you! I'll shut
this place down...!

21
00:01:58,285 --> 00:02:00,129
- Hey!
- Whoa, whoa, whoa.

22
00:02:00,153 --> 00:02:01,130
- Wait!
- Stop, stop, stop, stop.

23
00:02:01,154 --> 00:02:02,131
Please.

24
00:02:02,155 --> 00:02:03,132
It's over.

25
00:02:03,156 --> 00:02:04,767
No, it's not over.

26
00:02:04,791 --> 00:02:06,657
Believe me.

27
00:02:10,530 --> 00:02:12,965
I think he took that
rather poorly, don't you?

28
00:02:33,286 --> 00:02:34,797
Uh-uh.

29
00:02:34,821 --> 00:02:38,034
Whatever it is, it's going
to have to wait till morning.

30
00:02:38,058 --> 00:02:40,550
I'm going home.

31
00:03:08,588 --> 00:03:10,433
Oh, don't take this wrong, Stan,

32
00:03:10,457 --> 00:03:14,504
but whatever you
want, the answer is no.

33
00:03:14,528 --> 00:03:16,906
Well, that's a hell of a
way to greet your boss.

34
00:03:16,930 --> 00:03:18,675
But I can understand.

35
00:03:18,699 --> 00:03:21,310
I am genuinely sorry
about your vacation.

36
00:03:21,334 --> 00:03:22,311
I'll make it up.

37
00:03:22,335 --> 00:03:24,380
Thank you very much.

38
00:03:24,404 --> 00:03:27,216
How about starting right now,

39
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:29,452
by giving these
to somebody else.

40
00:03:29,476 --> 00:03:31,354
There is nobody else.

41
00:03:31,378 --> 00:03:33,210
In fact...

42
00:03:38,085 --> 00:03:39,629
What is this?

43
00:03:39,653 --> 00:03:41,030
The Richard Wagner murder case.

44
00:03:41,054 --> 00:03:46,002
Stan, the Martin trial
starts on the 18th.

45
00:03:46,026 --> 00:03:47,670
Julie, we're all overworked.

46
00:03:47,694 --> 00:03:49,572
But I have a dozen open cases.

47
00:03:49,596 --> 00:03:50,907
Tell it to the legislature.

48
00:03:50,931 --> 00:03:53,696
They're the ones
who cut the budget.

49
00:04:09,549 --> 00:04:11,961
No, I recommend that
you hang onto these stocks.

50
00:04:11,985 --> 00:04:14,511
They've shown steady growth
in the past three quarters.

51
00:04:16,656 --> 00:04:19,135
Lieutenant, couldn't we do
this after the market closes?

52
00:04:19,159 --> 00:04:20,923
I've just got a couple
more questions.

53
00:04:24,397 --> 00:04:27,143
As far as you know,

54
00:04:27,167 --> 00:04:29,645
did Richard Wagner
and Avery Campbell

55
00:04:29,669 --> 00:04:32,482
have any arguments prior to
the one they had last night?

56
00:04:32,506 --> 00:04:34,917
Richard was on
A.C.'s case a lot.

57
00:04:34,941 --> 00:04:37,987
I don't know if I'd
call them arguments.

58
00:04:38,011 --> 00:04:39,889
You have to understand
something, Lieutenant...

59
00:04:39,913 --> 00:04:44,460
A.C. is basically a sweetheart,
but he's had some problems.

60
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:45,862
What kind of problems?

61
00:04:45,886 --> 00:04:47,730
His wife died last year.

62
00:04:47,754 --> 00:04:49,365
He couldn't handle it.

63
00:04:49,389 --> 00:04:50,967
He became so depressed,

64
00:04:50,991 --> 00:04:53,369
they had to institutionalize
him for a month.

65
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:56,038
When he finally did
come back to work,

66
00:04:56,062 --> 00:04:58,407
well, he was never
really up to par after that.

67
00:04:58,431 --> 00:04:59,776
How could Mr. Wagner fire him?

68
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:01,043
Weren't they partners?

69
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:02,979
Richard bought A.C.
out two years ago,

70
00:05:03,003 --> 00:05:04,380
when his wife became ill.

71
00:05:04,404 --> 00:05:07,717
So you see, Lieutenant, A.C.
is just a salaried employee

72
00:05:07,741 --> 00:05:09,318
like the rest of us.

73
00:05:09,342 --> 00:05:11,587
Now, if you don't
mind, I really do have

74
00:05:11,611 --> 00:05:13,256
some very important
phone calls to make

75
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:16,292
to the West Coast, Lieutenant.

76
00:05:16,316 --> 00:05:22,031
Yeah, well, I want to thank
you for your time, and, uh,

77
00:05:22,055 --> 00:05:23,546
and I'll be...

78
00:05:25,192 --> 00:05:27,423
in touch.

79
00:05:28,428 --> 00:05:30,273
Hi.

80
00:05:30,297 --> 00:05:32,975
Hi, Julie.

81
00:05:32,999 --> 00:05:34,076
What you got?

82
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:36,212
I'm just about to question

83
00:05:36,236 --> 00:05:39,081
Avery Campbell...
The victim's ex-partner.

84
00:05:39,105 --> 00:05:41,217
- Care to...?
- Tag along?

85
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:42,607
You bet.

86
00:05:44,511 --> 00:05:48,791
And you left the
building at what time?

87
00:05:48,815 --> 00:05:52,094
7:15.

88
00:05:52,118 --> 00:05:55,231
Now I understand that
you and Mr. Wagner

89
00:05:55,255 --> 00:05:57,767
had a rather loud
argument just before that.

90
00:05:57,791 --> 00:06:00,837
Yes, we did.

91
00:06:00,861 --> 00:06:04,106
I-I'm not proud of my
behavior, Lieutenant.

92
00:06:04,130 --> 00:06:08,845
But Richard could be...

93
00:06:08,869 --> 00:06:10,701
quite aggravating at times.

94
00:06:12,172 --> 00:06:18,988
Well, I understand...
he was firing you.

95
00:06:19,012 --> 00:06:22,676
Yes, he was.

96
00:06:24,284 --> 00:06:28,264
Mr. Campbell, where did
you go after you left last night?

97
00:06:28,288 --> 00:06:30,633
Home.

98
00:06:30,657 --> 00:06:35,705
Uh, I made myself some
dinner, and, uh, went to bed.

99
00:06:35,729 --> 00:06:38,908
Did you talk to anybody?
Make any phone calls?

100
00:06:38,932 --> 00:06:42,011
Well, I tried to call my
son, but he wasn't home.

101
00:06:42,035 --> 00:06:44,800
Does anybody know
you were home all night?

102
00:06:46,706 --> 00:06:49,719
I'm a widower, Miss March.

103
00:06:49,743 --> 00:06:54,590
Living alone makes it hard
to come up with an alibi.

104
00:06:54,614 --> 00:06:58,551
Especially when you have no
idea you're going to need one.

105
00:07:01,688 --> 00:07:03,499
So you left the building at 7:15

106
00:07:03,523 --> 00:07:05,301
and didn't come
back till this morning...

107
00:07:05,325 --> 00:07:06,736
That what you're saying?

108
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:11,207
Yeah, that's correct... 7:15.

109
00:07:11,231 --> 00:07:14,377
8:30. I saw him there at 8:30.

110
00:07:14,401 --> 00:07:18,347
I know because that's
what time it always is

111
00:07:18,371 --> 00:07:21,083
when I finish emptying
the wastebaskets.

112
00:07:21,107 --> 00:07:23,352
That's what I was
doing when I saw him...

113
00:07:23,376 --> 00:07:24,620
Emptying the wastebaskets.

114
00:07:24,644 --> 00:07:26,789
And you're sure it
was Avery Campbell?

115
00:07:26,813 --> 00:07:29,191
Oh, yeah, it was Mr. Campbell.

116
00:07:29,215 --> 00:07:32,528
I know because whenever
he sees me he says hi.

117
00:07:32,552 --> 00:07:34,350
He's a nice man.

118
00:07:35,722 --> 00:07:39,568
I'm not getting him in
any kind of trouble, am I?

119
00:07:39,592 --> 00:07:41,737
No, don't worry. You've
been very helpful.

120
00:07:41,761 --> 00:07:42,972
And thanks.

121
00:07:42,996 --> 00:07:44,040
You can go now.

122
00:07:44,064 --> 00:07:46,226
Thank you very much.

123
00:07:49,936 --> 00:07:51,213
Hi, Bob.

124
00:07:51,237 --> 00:07:52,848
- Julie.
- Hi, Stan.

125
00:07:52,872 --> 00:07:55,551
So what's this I hear about a
witness in the Wagner case?

126
00:07:55,575 --> 00:07:57,620
The man who just left.

127
00:07:57,644 --> 00:07:59,822
He's a custodian at
the brokerage firm.

128
00:07:59,846 --> 00:08:01,057
He says he saw Avery Campbell

129
00:08:01,081 --> 00:08:03,693
in his office last
night at 8:30.

130
00:08:03,717 --> 00:08:06,495
Campbell told us he
was at home from 7:30 on.

131
00:08:06,519 --> 00:08:07,863
Well, there you go.

132
00:08:07,887 --> 00:08:09,253
When you going to file it?

133
00:08:10,991 --> 00:08:12,535
Is there a problem?

134
00:08:12,559 --> 00:08:16,806
I'm just not sure that Avery
Campbell is the man we're after.

135
00:08:16,830 --> 00:08:19,375
Julie, the man had motive,
means and opportunity.

136
00:08:19,399 --> 00:08:21,844
And he lied about
where he was last night.

137
00:08:21,868 --> 00:08:23,112
Well, of course he lied.

138
00:08:23,136 --> 00:08:24,880
He's afraid he'd be
accused of murder.

139
00:08:24,904 --> 00:08:26,782
He was afraid he'd get caught.

140
00:08:26,806 --> 00:08:30,886
Stan, we haven't even
found the murder weapon yet.

141
00:08:30,910 --> 00:08:34,890
And I just can't see
that nice, little old man

142
00:08:34,914 --> 00:08:36,246
as a killer.

143
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:44,121
Julie, file the indictment.

144
00:08:54,667 --> 00:08:56,145
Oh, Miss March.

145
00:08:56,169 --> 00:08:58,247
- Come along.
- Please, for just a moment.

146
00:08:58,271 --> 00:09:00,549
I'm sorry, Mr. Campbell,
but I can't talk to you

147
00:09:00,573 --> 00:09:02,151
without your lawyer present.

148
00:09:02,175 --> 00:09:04,320
I know. He told
me the same thing,

149
00:09:04,344 --> 00:09:05,988
but it isn't about this case.

150
00:09:06,012 --> 00:09:08,072
Please?

151
00:09:15,388 --> 00:09:18,367
I, uh, wanted you
to know why I lied

152
00:09:18,391 --> 00:09:21,771
about being in the
building that night.

153
00:09:21,795 --> 00:09:25,808
As you pointed out,
Richard was letting me go.

154
00:09:25,832 --> 00:09:29,478
That must have been
very painful for you.

155
00:09:29,502 --> 00:09:31,747
It was a great blow
to my pride, yes,

156
00:09:31,771 --> 00:09:34,750
but that... there's
more to it than that.

157
00:09:34,774 --> 00:09:39,922
You see, I'm not as well
off as you might think.

158
00:09:39,946 --> 00:09:43,859
I have a son who, um,

159
00:09:43,883 --> 00:09:46,562
hasn't found himself
professionally,

160
00:09:46,586 --> 00:09:47,763
if you know what I mean.

161
00:09:47,787 --> 00:09:49,165
You support him?

162
00:09:49,189 --> 00:09:53,702
Yes. And my wife's illness
was a terrible financial

163
00:09:53,726 --> 00:09:56,672
as well an emotional burden.

164
00:09:56,696 --> 00:09:58,841
I have to work.

165
00:09:58,865 --> 00:10:03,412
And... I lied...

166
00:10:03,436 --> 00:10:06,949
Because I didn't
want anyone to know

167
00:10:06,973 --> 00:10:09,272
what I was doing in
that building that night.

168
00:10:11,077 --> 00:10:16,392
I was, uh, making
copies of client lists.

169
00:10:16,416 --> 00:10:20,062
I thought they might
help me to get a job

170
00:10:20,086 --> 00:10:22,487
with another firm.

171
00:10:24,157 --> 00:10:28,857
I, uh, I just wanted
you to know.

172
00:10:30,029 --> 00:10:31,361
Thank you.

173
00:10:38,204 --> 00:10:39,815
Miss March, I understand

174
00:10:39,839 --> 00:10:42,151
you have a recommendation
concerning bail.

175
00:10:42,175 --> 00:10:43,986
Yes, I do, Your Honor.

176
00:10:44,010 --> 00:10:46,589
The state would like to
recommend that bail in this case

177
00:10:46,613 --> 00:10:49,125
be set at, uh, $10,000.

178
00:10:49,149 --> 00:10:51,093
$10,000, Miss March?

179
00:10:51,117 --> 00:10:53,829
Yes, Your Honor.

180
00:10:53,853 --> 00:10:56,565
Very well. So ordered.

181
00:10:57,757 --> 00:10:59,020
Next case.

182
00:11:11,004 --> 00:11:12,915
- Hi.
- Hi.

183
00:11:12,939 --> 00:11:14,884
What are you...?
Oh, these flowers.

184
00:11:14,908 --> 00:11:17,987
Our dinner!

185
00:11:18,011 --> 00:11:22,958
Oh, I guess we won't be having

186
00:11:22,982 --> 00:11:25,427
your famous fried
chicken dinner?

187
00:11:25,451 --> 00:11:28,764
Oh, Ben, oh, I'm so sorry.

188
00:11:28,788 --> 00:11:31,233
I've just been so swamped.

189
00:11:31,257 --> 00:11:33,802
I can barely remember my name.

190
00:11:33,826 --> 00:11:36,939
Okay, we'll go out.

191
00:11:36,963 --> 00:11:38,807
I don't think I have the energy.

192
00:11:38,831 --> 00:11:42,478
Okay, we'll stay in.

193
00:11:42,502 --> 00:11:44,113
I'll cook.

194
00:11:44,137 --> 00:11:45,748
There's nothing here.

195
00:11:45,772 --> 00:11:49,151
I don't even have time to go
to the grocery store anymore.

196
00:11:49,175 --> 00:11:50,686
Oh, I'll scrounge
around in here.

197
00:11:50,710 --> 00:11:52,688
There's bound to be something

198
00:11:52,712 --> 00:11:54,023
in the back of the refrigerator.

199
00:11:54,047 --> 00:11:56,425
Yes, but it's usually green.

200
00:11:56,449 --> 00:12:00,329
Stanley's working you too hard.

201
00:12:00,353 --> 00:12:01,630
It's not his fault.

202
00:12:01,654 --> 00:12:05,367
There's just too much
work and too few people.

203
00:12:05,391 --> 00:12:08,504
Anyway, I think I'm just mostly
upset about this Wagner case.

204
00:12:08,528 --> 00:12:09,505
Oh.

205
00:12:09,529 --> 00:12:11,974
I hear you got an indictment.

206
00:12:11,998 --> 00:12:12,975
Uh-huh.

207
00:12:12,999 --> 00:12:16,712
Avery Campbell,
Wagner's ex-partner.

208
00:12:16,736 --> 00:12:18,547
What's the problem?

209
00:12:18,571 --> 00:12:21,717
I don't know.

210
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:24,687
I just, uh...

211
00:12:24,711 --> 00:12:28,524
I just feel uncomfortable
prosecuting this case.

212
00:12:28,548 --> 00:12:30,059
I seem to be the only person

213
00:12:30,083 --> 00:12:31,794
who isn't sure Avery
Campbell did it.

214
00:12:31,818 --> 00:12:34,930
Well, you've indicted on less.

215
00:12:34,954 --> 00:12:36,832
I know.

216
00:12:36,856 --> 00:12:38,848
I just have this feeling.

217
00:12:41,027 --> 00:12:44,573
He's such a sweet man.

218
00:12:44,597 --> 00:12:46,675
You should've seen him
at the arraignment, Ben.

219
00:12:46,699 --> 00:12:50,546
He was so frightened
and so overwhelmed.

220
00:12:50,570 --> 00:12:55,351
Well, you've still got time.

221
00:12:55,375 --> 00:12:57,019
Why don't you keep digging,

222
00:12:57,043 --> 00:13:00,422
and then, if
you're not satisfied

223
00:13:00,446 --> 00:13:03,926
and still don't like
it when trial comes,

224
00:13:03,950 --> 00:13:06,929
you just have Stanley
give it to somebody else.

225
00:13:06,953 --> 00:13:08,597
There is nobody else.

226
00:13:08,621 --> 00:13:10,613
No?

227
00:13:13,393 --> 00:13:14,861
You want to set the table?

228
00:13:16,696 --> 00:13:18,407
What'd you find?

229
00:13:18,431 --> 00:13:19,842
Oh.

230
00:13:19,866 --> 00:13:22,645
Some eggs and some milk

231
00:13:22,669 --> 00:13:26,315
and-and some onions
and a little piece of cheese.

232
00:13:26,339 --> 00:13:27,716
Good omelet.

233
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:29,084
You're a genius.

234
00:13:30,943 --> 00:13:32,935
Hello.

235
00:13:35,815 --> 00:13:36,976
Oh, no.

236
00:13:43,356 --> 00:13:45,734
Yes, I will.

237
00:13:45,758 --> 00:13:47,021
Thank you for calling.

238
00:13:51,397 --> 00:13:52,456
What's wrong?

239
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:57,179
Avery Campbell just
committed suicide.

240
00:13:57,203 --> 00:14:00,435
Oh, Julie, I'm so sorry.

241
00:14:02,475 --> 00:14:07,856
I... I shouldn't have let Stan

242
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:10,793
push me into filing
that indictment.

243
00:14:10,817 --> 00:14:12,795
Well, you didn't know
this would happen.

244
00:14:12,819 --> 00:14:13,796
No.

245
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:15,597
Yes, I did. I knew.

246
00:14:15,621 --> 00:14:19,401
I knew he couldn't handle this.

247
00:14:19,425 --> 00:14:20,903
I let it happen.

248
00:14:20,927 --> 00:14:22,953
Julie, it's not your fault.

249
00:14:25,832 --> 00:14:27,824
Julie.

250
00:14:58,698 --> 00:14:59,908
Oh, morning, Bob.

251
00:14:59,932 --> 00:15:00,909
Oh, hi, Ben.

252
00:15:00,933 --> 00:15:02,311
What are you doing here?

253
00:15:02,335 --> 00:15:04,380
Julie.

254
00:15:04,404 --> 00:15:05,381
You about done?

255
00:15:05,405 --> 00:15:06,615
Yeah.

256
00:15:06,639 --> 00:15:08,817
Been here all night.

257
00:15:08,841 --> 00:15:11,320
Comes up your
garden-variety suicide.

258
00:15:11,344 --> 00:15:13,489
One shot, close
range, right temple.

259
00:15:13,513 --> 00:15:15,157
Found this gun in his hand.

260
00:15:15,181 --> 00:15:16,325
Oh.

261
00:15:16,349 --> 00:15:18,614
This note was found
in his typewriter.

262
00:15:20,653 --> 00:15:22,731
Admits killing Wagner, huh?

263
00:15:22,755 --> 00:15:24,333
Says dying is preferable

264
00:15:24,357 --> 00:15:26,935
to spending the rest
of his life in prison.

265
00:15:26,959 --> 00:15:28,871
His son I.D.'d the gun.

266
00:15:28,895 --> 00:15:30,973
Said Campbell kept it in
the top drawer of his desk.

267
00:15:30,997 --> 00:15:32,989
Thanks.

268
00:15:36,969 --> 00:15:38,113
Excuse me.

269
00:15:38,137 --> 00:15:40,682
I'm Julie March
with the D.A.'s Office.

270
00:15:40,706 --> 00:15:41,784
Paul Campbell.

271
00:15:41,808 --> 00:15:45,187
I'm so sorry about your father.

272
00:15:45,211 --> 00:15:47,389
I-I just don't understand.

273
00:15:47,413 --> 00:15:50,159
When I saw him last
night, he seemed fine.

274
00:15:50,183 --> 00:15:51,760
You were here last night?

275
00:15:51,784 --> 00:15:53,595
Dropping off his car.

276
00:15:53,619 --> 00:15:56,365
I borrowed it last Sunday.

277
00:15:56,389 --> 00:15:58,500
He said he needed
it for a couple days,

278
00:15:58,524 --> 00:16:00,602
probably just to
clean out his office,

279
00:16:00,626 --> 00:16:03,605
and then I'd be
welcome to keep it.

280
00:16:05,765 --> 00:16:07,976
He even said
that he'd drop it off

281
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,880
so I could take it to a
job interview on Friday.

282
00:16:11,904 --> 00:16:14,917
I just, I just don't understand.

283
00:16:14,941 --> 00:16:16,933
I'm so sorry.

284
00:16:18,945 --> 00:16:20,322
Where did that come from?

285
00:16:20,346 --> 00:16:23,459
Oh, just found it in the
trunk of Avery Campbell's car.

286
00:16:23,483 --> 00:16:24,927
Remember seeing one just like it

287
00:16:24,951 --> 00:16:27,696
in the conference room
at the brokerage house.

288
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:28,964
Think it's the murder weapon?

289
00:16:28,988 --> 00:16:31,633
But Richard Wagner was
killed on Monday night.

290
00:16:31,657 --> 00:16:32,835
Yeah?

291
00:16:32,859 --> 00:16:35,938
Avery Campbell
didn't have his car then.

292
00:16:35,962 --> 00:16:38,874
His son just told me he
borrowed it on Sunday

293
00:16:38,898 --> 00:16:41,477
and didn't return
it until last night.

294
00:16:41,501 --> 00:16:43,011
Yeah?

295
00:16:43,035 --> 00:16:45,747
How did that candlestick
get in his trunk?

296
00:16:45,771 --> 00:16:48,741
He probably put it in
there after his son left.

297
00:16:54,347 --> 00:16:55,491
Julie?

298
00:16:55,515 --> 00:16:57,359
I'll see you.

299
00:16:57,383 --> 00:16:58,874
Julie?

300
00:17:00,887 --> 00:17:01,864
Julie!

301
00:17:01,888 --> 00:17:04,266
Why would Avery Campbell

302
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:07,636
put that candlestick
in his car last night?

303
00:17:07,660 --> 00:17:09,671
Well, I don't know.

304
00:17:09,695 --> 00:17:11,640
He was gonna
get rid of it, I guess.

305
00:17:11,664 --> 00:17:14,810
Why bother? He was going
to commit suicide, remember?

306
00:17:14,834 --> 00:17:18,146
Well, maybe-maybe
he hadn't decided yet.

307
00:17:18,170 --> 00:17:19,281
I don't think so.

308
00:17:19,305 --> 00:17:21,817
The only way this
makes any sense at all

309
00:17:21,841 --> 00:17:25,821
is if somebody shot him,
put the gun in his hand,

310
00:17:25,845 --> 00:17:29,291
typed that note and then
planted the candlestick

311
00:17:29,315 --> 00:17:32,227
in his car, not knowing that
his son had just returned it.

312
00:17:32,251 --> 00:17:33,729
- Well...
- And the only person

313
00:17:33,753 --> 00:17:36,164
who would've had the candlestick

314
00:17:36,188 --> 00:17:39,668
is the person who really
murdered Richard Wagner.

315
00:17:39,692 --> 00:17:41,203
Don't you see?

316
00:17:41,227 --> 00:17:45,574
He killed Avery Campbell
and made it look like a suicide

317
00:17:45,598 --> 00:17:48,777
so that everybody would
think both cases were closed.

318
00:17:48,801 --> 00:17:51,613
Well, it-it sounds okay,

319
00:17:51,637 --> 00:17:53,482
but it could be that
you're, you know,

320
00:17:53,506 --> 00:17:55,551
just hoping and
jumping to conclusions.

321
00:17:55,575 --> 00:17:57,553
Aren't you the one
who's always saying

322
00:17:57,577 --> 00:17:59,621
you got to go
with your instincts?

323
00:17:59,645 --> 00:18:01,023
Well, yeah.

324
00:18:01,047 --> 00:18:04,192
I may have said something
to that effect once or twice.

325
00:18:04,216 --> 00:18:06,395
Well, my instincts
are telling me

326
00:18:06,419 --> 00:18:09,097
that Avery Campbell
did not commit suicide.

327
00:18:09,121 --> 00:18:12,614
And, by George, this time
I'm gonna listen to them.

328
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:21,944
Ms. Cole?

329
00:18:21,968 --> 00:18:25,047
I'm too old to be
liberated, honey.

330
00:18:25,071 --> 00:18:26,648
"Miss" is fine.

331
00:18:26,672 --> 00:18:27,950
Betty is even better.

332
00:18:27,974 --> 00:18:29,818
Well, I'm Julie.

333
00:18:29,842 --> 00:18:31,787
Julie March with
the D.A.'s Office.

334
00:18:31,811 --> 00:18:33,622
May I talk to you for a minute?

335
00:18:33,646 --> 00:18:34,670
Sure.

336
00:18:36,716 --> 00:18:38,994
My goodness.

337
00:18:39,018 --> 00:18:41,463
Mr. Wagner's
office was really...

338
00:18:41,487 --> 00:18:42,898
Pretentious.

339
00:18:42,922 --> 00:18:45,734
That's the word most
people used to use.

340
00:18:45,758 --> 00:18:48,103
Behind his back, of course.

341
00:18:48,127 --> 00:18:52,207
Ms. Cole, I under... Betty.

342
00:18:52,231 --> 00:18:53,942
I understand that Mr. Wagner

343
00:18:53,966 --> 00:18:56,311
was not an easy
man to get along with.

344
00:18:56,335 --> 00:18:57,446
Is that true?

345
00:18:57,470 --> 00:19:00,248
About as true as you can get.

346
00:19:00,272 --> 00:19:03,385
So he had a lot of enemies?

347
00:19:03,409 --> 00:19:07,623
Well, he sure didn't
have a lot of friends.

348
00:19:07,647 --> 00:19:10,626
She's very pretty.

349
00:19:10,650 --> 00:19:13,261
His daughter?

350
00:19:13,285 --> 00:19:14,963
Hardly.

351
00:19:14,987 --> 00:19:17,332
That's the third Mrs. Wagner.

352
00:19:17,356 --> 00:19:18,467
Celeste.

353
00:19:18,491 --> 00:19:19,468
Oh.

354
00:19:19,492 --> 00:19:21,703
"Oh" is right.

355
00:19:21,727 --> 00:19:24,973
I guess I'm lucky he didn't
trade me in for a younger model.

356
00:19:24,997 --> 00:19:27,009
You were his secretary
for a long time?

357
00:19:27,033 --> 00:19:28,076
Mm-hmm.

358
00:19:28,100 --> 00:19:30,078
Since before the
firm even existed.

359
00:19:30,102 --> 00:19:34,650
Then you must know everything
that goes on around here.

360
00:19:34,674 --> 00:19:38,270
Honey, if I don't know
it, it isn't worth knowing.

361
00:19:39,512 --> 00:19:40,822
I really don't understand

362
00:19:40,846 --> 00:19:42,257
what it is you're
investigating, Miss March.

363
00:19:42,281 --> 00:19:43,992
I thought that
A.C. had left a note

364
00:19:44,016 --> 00:19:45,961
saying that he was the
one who had killed Richard.

365
00:19:45,985 --> 00:19:47,596
Yes, but there are
still a few things

366
00:19:47,620 --> 00:19:48,897
that need to be cleared up.

367
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:49,931
Like what?

368
00:19:49,955 --> 00:19:52,134
Well, like where
you were at 9:00

369
00:19:52,158 --> 00:19:53,568
on the night of the murder.

370
00:19:53,592 --> 00:19:54,936
Jogging.

371
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:56,672
Do you always jog at night?

372
00:19:56,696 --> 00:19:59,775
I either jog then or
go to the gym, yes.

373
00:19:59,799 --> 00:20:01,176
Anything else?

374
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:04,646
I understand Avery Campbell
wasn't the only person

375
00:20:04,670 --> 00:20:07,504
who'd been having problems
with Mr. Wagner lately.

376
00:20:09,542 --> 00:20:11,586
Yes, I had problems
with Richard.

377
00:20:11,610 --> 00:20:13,355
From the very beginning.

378
00:20:13,379 --> 00:20:15,290
At first it was because
I was a woman...

379
00:20:15,314 --> 00:20:18,160
Which meant, in his eyes, I
was only good for two things,

380
00:20:18,184 --> 00:20:20,195
one of which was
fetching his coffee.

381
00:20:20,219 --> 00:20:21,697
What about recently?

382
00:20:22,755 --> 00:20:24,132
Last quarter, I
earned my clients

383
00:20:24,156 --> 00:20:26,234
more money than any
other broker in this company.

384
00:20:26,258 --> 00:20:29,237
I felt I deserved to
become a partner.

385
00:20:29,261 --> 00:20:31,907
Richard didn't agree.

386
00:20:31,931 --> 00:20:32,941
Anything else?

387
00:20:32,965 --> 00:20:34,176
No, no.

388
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:35,677
I think that's all.

389
00:20:35,701 --> 00:20:36,845
Good.

390
00:20:36,869 --> 00:20:38,313
Because I'm about
to make my client

391
00:20:38,337 --> 00:20:39,965
several thousand dollars richer.

392
00:20:41,974 --> 00:20:43,418
David. Taylor.

393
00:20:43,442 --> 00:20:44,419
Yes, listen.

394
00:20:44,443 --> 00:20:45,821
BNX Oil just hit 27.

395
00:20:45,845 --> 00:20:48,974
I want you to sell
all 5,000 shares <i>now.</i>

396
00:20:50,015 --> 00:20:52,828
Mr. Randall!

397
00:20:52,852 --> 00:20:53,852
Hi.

398
00:20:54,987 --> 00:20:57,365
I'm Julie March
with the D.A.'s Office.

399
00:20:57,389 --> 00:21:00,035
I was hoping you'd have
time to answer a few questions.

400
00:21:00,059 --> 00:21:01,503
On my way to a lunch meeting.

401
00:21:01,527 --> 00:21:02,604
Oh, well, maybe later.

402
00:21:02,628 --> 00:21:03,905
What time will you be back?

403
00:21:03,929 --> 00:21:06,708
I'm not really sure.

404
00:21:06,732 --> 00:21:08,009
Questions about what?

405
00:21:08,033 --> 00:21:10,412
Your relationship to
Richard Wagner mostly.

406
00:21:10,436 --> 00:21:13,281
He was my mentor,
and I'll miss him dearly.

407
00:21:13,305 --> 00:21:14,683
If you have any more questions,

408
00:21:14,707 --> 00:21:16,585
my secretary will have
to set something up.

409
00:21:16,609 --> 00:21:19,135
Excuse me.

410
00:21:25,584 --> 00:21:27,596
Julie.

411
00:21:27,620 --> 00:21:28,697
What's going on?

412
00:21:28,721 --> 00:21:29,731
What do you mean?

413
00:21:29,755 --> 00:21:31,199
I hear this morning
you asked for

414
00:21:31,223 --> 00:21:32,601
a continuance in
the Martin case.

415
00:21:32,625 --> 00:21:34,603
Yes, I need more
time to prepare.

416
00:21:34,627 --> 00:21:36,138
Well, if you weren't
out harassing people,

417
00:21:36,162 --> 00:21:38,240
you'd have plenty
of time to prepare.

418
00:21:38,264 --> 00:21:40,175
I'm not harassing anybody.

419
00:21:40,199 --> 00:21:41,977
Well, that's not what
John Randall says.

420
00:21:42,001 --> 00:21:44,513
You know, frankly, Julie,
I don't understand you.

421
00:21:44,537 --> 00:21:47,082
One minute, you're telling
me how overworked you are,

422
00:21:47,106 --> 00:21:48,917
and the next minute
you're wasting your time,

423
00:21:48,941 --> 00:21:50,619
to say nothing of
taxpayer dollars,

424
00:21:50,643 --> 00:21:52,754
over a case that's
already been closed.

425
00:21:52,778 --> 00:21:54,756
But it shouldn't be closed.

426
00:21:54,780 --> 00:21:57,659
I'm almost positive Avery
Campbell was murdered.

427
00:21:57,683 --> 00:21:59,628
Avery Campbell
committed suicide.

428
00:21:59,652 --> 00:22:00,629
No, no, no.

429
00:22:00,653 --> 00:22:02,798
He was murdered
by the same person

430
00:22:02,822 --> 00:22:03,799
who murdered Richard Wagner.

431
00:22:03,823 --> 00:22:04,800
Oh, Julie,

432
00:22:04,824 --> 00:22:06,268
- will you please...
- Then why would

433
00:22:06,292 --> 00:22:08,170
he commit suicide
just hours after

434
00:22:08,194 --> 00:22:10,138
he insisted to me
that he was innocent?

435
00:22:10,162 --> 00:22:12,274
Because the man was
emotionally unstable, that's why.

436
00:22:12,298 --> 00:22:13,775
For God's sake,
look at the facts.

437
00:22:13,799 --> 00:22:14,776
I have.

438
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:17,112
I have, and I'm not convinced.

439
00:22:17,136 --> 00:22:18,914
Julie, I know you liked
this man Avery Campbell,

440
00:22:18,938 --> 00:22:21,082
but I'm here to tell
you, once and for all,

441
00:22:21,106 --> 00:22:23,752
that the Wagner and
Campbell cases are closed.

442
00:22:23,776 --> 00:22:26,354
- Stan...
- You know as well as I do

443
00:22:26,378 --> 00:22:29,825
that sweet little old men
commit murder every day.

444
00:22:29,849 --> 00:22:33,829
Now, I want you to stop
taking these continuances

445
00:22:33,853 --> 00:22:36,331
and get on with your work.

446
00:22:36,355 --> 00:22:37,355
Am I clear?

447
00:22:38,390 --> 00:22:40,325
Yes.

448
00:22:46,832 --> 00:22:48,276
<i>You should've seen him.</i>

449
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:49,477
Stan?

450
00:22:49,501 --> 00:22:52,147
I felt like I was
in first grade,

451
00:22:52,171 --> 00:22:55,083
getting bawled out
for talking out of turn.

452
00:22:56,675 --> 00:22:58,353
I got bawled out one time.

453
00:22:58,377 --> 00:23:00,288
Actually, I got sent home.

454
00:23:00,312 --> 00:23:04,893
We had a teacher, and one
morning she gave us a test

455
00:23:04,917 --> 00:23:08,063
that was so hard
that everybody failed.

456
00:23:08,087 --> 00:23:09,464
Everybody.

457
00:23:09,488 --> 00:23:12,868
So a bunch of us boys got it up
that next time she turned around

458
00:23:12,892 --> 00:23:15,437
at the blackboard, we was
gonna throw spitballs at her.

459
00:23:15,461 --> 00:23:17,906
Well, I had, I had a big one.

460
00:23:17,930 --> 00:23:19,241
Good one.

461
00:23:19,265 --> 00:23:22,811
When she turned
around, I reared back

462
00:23:22,835 --> 00:23:23,879
and let go.

463
00:23:23,903 --> 00:23:25,480
And I was the
only one that threw.

464
00:23:25,504 --> 00:23:26,548
Caught her right on the butt.

465
00:23:26,572 --> 00:23:28,083
Just splat!

466
00:23:29,575 --> 00:23:33,021
She knew it was me 'cause I
was the only one that was grinning.

467
00:23:33,045 --> 00:23:34,556
Mama gave me a whipping.

468
00:23:34,580 --> 00:23:36,258
But it was funny.

469
00:23:36,282 --> 00:23:38,460
I mean, just splat,
right on the butt.

470
00:23:39,985 --> 00:23:41,630
You got any napkins?

471
00:23:41,654 --> 00:23:42,631
Here.

472
00:23:42,655 --> 00:23:43,932
Yeah, thanks.

473
00:23:43,956 --> 00:23:44,933
Want half?

474
00:23:44,957 --> 00:23:46,368
Oh, no, I'm not hungry.

475
00:23:46,392 --> 00:23:47,769
My mustard.

476
00:23:47,793 --> 00:23:49,571
Mustard, mustard, mustard.

477
00:23:49,595 --> 00:23:50,839
Just splat!

478
00:23:53,732 --> 00:23:57,445
I can't believe that
John Randall called Stan.

479
00:23:57,469 --> 00:23:59,247
Maybe he was upset.

480
00:23:59,271 --> 00:24:01,016
I barely spoke to the man.

481
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:04,920
But he called Stan and
says that I'm harassing him.

482
00:24:04,944 --> 00:24:06,288
Now why would he do that?

483
00:24:06,312 --> 00:24:08,423
Does he have something to hide?

484
00:24:08,447 --> 00:24:09,991
Stan doesn't think so.

485
00:24:10,015 --> 00:24:14,062
Stan thinks Avery Campbell
didn't want to get fired.

486
00:24:14,086 --> 00:24:15,964
Ben, what am I going to do?

487
00:24:15,988 --> 00:24:17,899
This thing is making me crazy!

488
00:24:17,923 --> 00:24:20,936
Well, ordinarily, I'd tell you

489
00:24:20,960 --> 00:24:23,371
to do what your
heart makes you do,

490
00:24:23,395 --> 00:24:25,974
but this time I can't.

491
00:24:25,998 --> 00:24:27,776
Why not?

492
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:34,049
I don't want you to get
involved with a two-time killer.

493
00:24:34,073 --> 00:24:35,684
Conrad isn't doing anything.

494
00:24:35,708 --> 00:24:37,786
He could, he could
give you a hand.

495
00:24:37,810 --> 00:24:40,155
That's very sweet of you.

496
00:24:40,179 --> 00:24:42,157
If I need help, I'll ask you.

497
00:24:42,181 --> 00:24:45,226
So you're gonna keep digging?

498
00:24:45,250 --> 00:24:46,895
That's what you do, isn't it?

499
00:24:46,919 --> 00:24:51,399
Well, yeah, yeah.

500
00:24:51,423 --> 00:24:53,969
And there-there's one-one
more thing I'd do if I were you.

501
00:24:53,993 --> 00:24:56,271
What?

502
00:24:56,295 --> 00:24:59,424
I'd put some mustard
on that sandwich.

503
00:25:08,974 --> 00:25:11,500
Yeah, yeah.

504
00:25:13,779 --> 00:25:17,325
Look, I spend hundreds of
dollars a month in your shop.

505
00:25:17,349 --> 00:25:20,128
I don't think it's
unreasonable for me to expect

506
00:25:20,152 --> 00:25:21,262
that the flowers you deliver

507
00:25:21,286 --> 00:25:25,633
to last longer than
24 hours, do you?

508
00:25:25,657 --> 00:25:28,286
Good. See that you do.

509
00:25:30,129 --> 00:25:31,873
Didn't your boss speak
to you, Miss March?

510
00:25:31,897 --> 00:25:33,541
As a matter of fact, he did.

511
00:25:33,565 --> 00:25:38,913
It was never my intention
to harass you, Mr. Randall.

512
00:25:38,937 --> 00:25:41,850
I just wanted to ask
you a few questions.

513
00:25:45,277 --> 00:25:46,745
Yes?

514
00:25:49,815 --> 00:25:52,560
I'll have to call him
back in five minutes.

515
00:25:52,584 --> 00:25:54,963
Make it quick.

516
00:25:54,987 --> 00:25:57,298
I see you've taken
over Mr. Wagner's office.

517
00:25:57,322 --> 00:25:59,434
You must be the new CEO.

518
00:25:59,458 --> 00:26:01,636
As of last night.

519
00:26:01,660 --> 00:26:03,838
Unanimous vote of
the board of directors.

520
00:26:03,862 --> 00:26:06,641
As I said, Richard taught
me everything I know.

521
00:26:06,665 --> 00:26:09,210
It was only natural that I
take over the company.

522
00:26:09,234 --> 00:26:10,845
Oh, I'm sorry.

523
00:26:10,869 --> 00:26:13,248
I-I heard things between you two

524
00:26:13,272 --> 00:26:15,617
had been a little
strained lately.

525
00:26:15,641 --> 00:26:20,121
Something about a big
argument around three weeks ago.

526
00:26:20,145 --> 00:26:21,656
What was that about, anyway?

527
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:25,794
A couple of my clients
had taken some losses

528
00:26:25,818 --> 00:26:28,496
which Richard thought
could have been avoided.

529
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:30,198
I disagreed.

530
00:26:30,222 --> 00:26:32,033
It's no big deal.

531
00:26:32,057 --> 00:26:33,601
So it wasn't as
though you were about

532
00:26:33,625 --> 00:26:34,602
to get fired or anything.

533
00:26:34,626 --> 00:26:36,771
What are you trying to suggest?!

534
00:26:36,795 --> 00:26:39,074
That I killed Richard
to save my job?

535
00:26:39,098 --> 00:26:40,341
That's ridiculous!

536
00:26:40,365 --> 00:26:42,677
Where were you around
9:00 the night of the murder?

537
00:26:42,701 --> 00:26:43,945
Oh, please, please!

538
00:26:43,969 --> 00:26:46,514
I was in my car en route
to a groundbreaking party

539
00:26:46,538 --> 00:26:49,617
up in, uh, Rosemead.

540
00:26:49,641 --> 00:26:51,786
Did you take a date?

541
00:26:51,810 --> 00:26:54,622
I think I went alone.

542
00:26:54,646 --> 00:26:55,990
Oh.

543
00:26:57,916 --> 00:27:00,195
Yes?

544
00:27:00,219 --> 00:27:02,564
All right.

545
00:27:02,588 --> 00:27:04,365
You'll have to excuse me.

546
00:27:04,389 --> 00:27:07,168
It's imperative
that I take this call.

547
00:27:07,192 --> 00:27:10,424
Well, thank you for your time.

548
00:27:12,731 --> 00:27:14,275
And congratulations.

549
00:27:14,299 --> 00:27:17,269
Things seem to have
worked out pretty well for you.

550
00:27:26,111 --> 00:27:27,655
I just need you to switch

551
00:27:27,679 --> 00:27:29,491
these two paragraphs, all right?

552
00:27:29,515 --> 00:27:31,746
And I need it in ten minutes.

553
00:27:40,692 --> 00:27:44,038
You work for
Taylor Sinclair now?

554
00:27:44,062 --> 00:27:47,909
Well, don't look so
surprised, honey.

555
00:27:47,933 --> 00:27:50,979
Appropriating the
former CEO's secretary

556
00:27:51,003 --> 00:27:52,714
is a smart career move.

557
00:27:52,738 --> 00:27:54,048
How's by you?

558
00:27:54,072 --> 00:27:57,018
I just had a talk
with John Randall,

559
00:27:57,042 --> 00:27:59,354
and he seems to think
everything between him

560
00:27:59,378 --> 00:28:01,389
and Mr. Wagner
was just hunky-dory.

561
00:28:01,413 --> 00:28:04,459
Well, I don't know whether
he's lying to you or to himself,

562
00:28:04,483 --> 00:28:05,960
but believe me,

563
00:28:05,984 --> 00:28:09,197
if Richard had lived, John
would be history by now.

564
00:28:09,221 --> 00:28:11,966
That big argument they
had, he says it was about

565
00:28:11,990 --> 00:28:15,770
a couple of bad
investments that he'd made.

566
00:28:15,794 --> 00:28:18,773
No way. It was about
his coming in late,

567
00:28:18,797 --> 00:28:22,443
leaving early and all those
three-hour lunches in between.

568
00:28:22,467 --> 00:28:23,594
Bet on it.

569
00:28:26,772 --> 00:28:29,050
May I ask you one more thing?

570
00:28:29,074 --> 00:28:30,351
Sure.

571
00:28:30,375 --> 00:28:32,320
Do you happen to know the name

572
00:28:32,344 --> 00:28:33,788
of the florist John
Randall uses?

573
00:28:33,812 --> 00:28:36,424
You planning on
sending him roses?

574
00:29:07,412 --> 00:29:09,677
♪♪

575
00:29:18,824 --> 00:29:21,316
- Hi.
- Hi.

576
00:29:22,995 --> 00:29:24,806
Is this a stakeout?

577
00:29:24,830 --> 00:29:25,974
Uh-huh.

578
00:29:25,998 --> 00:29:30,778
Well, when did you
get into this line of work?

579
00:29:30,802 --> 00:29:33,448
When I couldn't
get anybody to help.

580
00:29:33,472 --> 00:29:35,350
What are you doing here?

581
00:29:35,374 --> 00:29:38,086
Conrad couldn't make it.

582
00:29:38,110 --> 00:29:39,754
You said he wasn't busy.

583
00:29:39,778 --> 00:29:40,855
Well, he is.

584
00:29:40,879 --> 00:29:42,624
What's up?

585
00:29:42,648 --> 00:29:45,593
I'm going to visit John Randall.

586
00:29:45,617 --> 00:29:47,795
Is he dangerous?

587
00:29:47,819 --> 00:29:49,597
I don't know.

588
00:29:49,621 --> 00:29:52,800
You just offered
me Conrad as a way

589
00:29:52,824 --> 00:29:55,069
of checking up
on me, didn't you?

590
00:29:55,093 --> 00:29:57,405
Yeah.

591
00:29:57,429 --> 00:29:59,140
Are you worried about me?

592
00:29:59,164 --> 00:30:01,895
Uh, a little.

593
00:30:05,237 --> 00:30:06,981
Thank you.

594
00:30:07,005 --> 00:30:08,098
Look!

595
00:30:16,148 --> 00:30:18,326
Daggone, he's
already going inside.

596
00:30:18,350 --> 00:30:19,794
That's okay.

597
00:30:19,818 --> 00:30:22,897
There must be 50
apartments in this building.

598
00:30:22,921 --> 00:30:24,766
He's in number 32.

599
00:30:24,790 --> 00:30:26,868
How'd you know that?

600
00:30:26,892 --> 00:30:29,203
Because he's had flowers
delivered there once a week

601
00:30:29,227 --> 00:30:31,719
for over three months. Come on.

602
00:30:36,702 --> 00:30:38,680
Hi.

603
00:30:38,704 --> 00:30:39,747
Yes?

604
00:30:39,771 --> 00:30:42,583
Could we speak to
John Randall, please?

605
00:30:42,607 --> 00:30:44,986
There's nobody
here by that name.

606
00:30:45,010 --> 00:30:46,954
Don't I know you?

607
00:30:46,978 --> 00:30:48,489
I don't think so.

608
00:30:48,513 --> 00:30:50,625
Uh, we-we just
want to talk to him.

609
00:30:50,649 --> 00:30:52,660
I don't know any John Randall.

610
00:30:52,684 --> 00:30:55,797
I remember. Your picture
was in Richard Wagner's office.

611
00:30:55,821 --> 00:30:58,299
If you don't leave, I'm
going to call the police.

612
00:30:58,323 --> 00:30:59,534
Now wait a minute!

613
00:30:59,558 --> 00:31:01,049
You were his wife.

614
00:31:03,462 --> 00:31:05,158
Let them in, Celeste.

615
00:31:10,936 --> 00:31:13,581
So now you know.

616
00:31:13,605 --> 00:31:16,751
More importantly, did
Richard Wagner know?

617
00:31:16,775 --> 00:31:21,723
He found out about
three weeks ago.

618
00:31:21,747 --> 00:31:23,758
That's what that big
argument was about.

619
00:31:23,782 --> 00:31:26,427
He discovered you were
having an affair with his wife.

620
00:31:26,451 --> 00:31:28,496
Doesn't change the
fact that I didn't kill him.

621
00:31:28,520 --> 00:31:30,765
But it gives you
an excellent motive.

622
00:31:30,789 --> 00:31:34,402
Why do you think I pushed
so hard to get you to lay off?

623
00:31:34,426 --> 00:31:36,003
He's telling the truth.

624
00:31:36,027 --> 00:31:39,073
We didn't plan on anything
to happen between us.

625
00:31:39,097 --> 00:31:40,908
It just did.

626
00:31:40,932 --> 00:31:42,677
And now your husband is dead.

627
00:31:42,701 --> 00:31:45,079
And instead of
being out of a job,

628
00:31:45,103 --> 00:31:46,981
your lover's running
the company.

629
00:31:47,005 --> 00:31:50,952
Couldn't have worked out
better if you'd planned it, could it?

630
00:31:50,976 --> 00:31:54,789
I was here the night Richard
was murdered with Celeste.

631
00:31:54,813 --> 00:31:57,992
Mr. Randall, in my experience
as a defense attorney,

632
00:31:58,016 --> 00:32:00,895
rarely does having your
mistress vouch for you,

633
00:32:00,919 --> 00:32:03,197
and say that you didn't
murder her husband,

634
00:32:03,221 --> 00:32:05,500
go over well with a jury.

635
00:32:05,524 --> 00:32:08,469
You don't have to
take our word for it.

636
00:32:08,493 --> 00:32:10,538
That was the night
we had a problem

637
00:32:10,562 --> 00:32:12,140
with the disposal, remember?

638
00:32:12,164 --> 00:32:13,875
That's right.

639
00:32:13,899 --> 00:32:17,011
The manager was here till
almost 10:00 trying to fix it.

640
00:32:17,035 --> 00:32:20,369
John's not very handy
around the house.

641
00:32:28,380 --> 00:32:30,091
Well, they're clear.

642
00:32:30,115 --> 00:32:31,459
Yep, sure are.

643
00:32:31,483 --> 00:32:32,627
John Randall didn't do it.

644
00:32:32,651 --> 00:32:34,462
Nope, he sure didn't.

645
00:32:34,486 --> 00:32:37,598
The real killer's still
out there somewhere.

646
00:32:37,622 --> 00:32:39,716
Yep, she sure is.

647
00:32:43,261 --> 00:32:45,821
She say "she"?

648
00:32:50,969 --> 00:32:52,346
Oh, thank you.

649
00:32:52,370 --> 00:32:53,929
Pardon me.

650
00:33:01,713 --> 00:33:02,857
Hi.

651
00:33:02,881 --> 00:33:04,892
Well, hello there.

652
00:33:04,916 --> 00:33:07,762
You're getting to be quite a
regular customer, aren't you?

653
00:33:07,786 --> 00:33:09,664
I know, and I apologize,

654
00:33:09,688 --> 00:33:13,868
but where would I find a
record of all the investments...

655
00:33:13,892 --> 00:33:16,871
that, uh, Taylor has made

656
00:33:16,895 --> 00:33:20,541
on behalf of her clients
in the last six months?

657
00:33:20,565 --> 00:33:23,160
Right there.

658
00:33:26,705 --> 00:33:29,884
You think you could
pull those files for me?

659
00:33:29,908 --> 00:33:32,571
Wait a minute, honey.

660
00:33:35,514 --> 00:33:37,592
This ought to keep
you off the hook.

661
00:33:37,616 --> 00:33:39,827
A subpoena!

662
00:33:41,520 --> 00:33:44,966
You like to play
hardball, don't you?

663
00:33:44,990 --> 00:33:48,222
Well, batter up.

664
00:33:49,728 --> 00:33:53,665
Still, I remember when you
were talking to me about it.

665
00:34:02,507 --> 00:34:05,253
Taylor seems to have
put most of her clients

666
00:34:05,277 --> 00:34:10,758
into Sheldrake Electronics,
Maystream Sciences and Raydyne

667
00:34:10,782 --> 00:34:12,293
at one time or another.

668
00:34:12,317 --> 00:34:16,898
And done very well by them.

669
00:34:16,922 --> 00:34:21,235
Well, Miss Sinclair's either
very smart or very lucky

670
00:34:21,259 --> 00:34:23,558
or very well-connected.

671
00:34:51,823 --> 00:34:53,267
Ben!

672
00:34:53,291 --> 00:34:54,702
Did you find out anything?

673
00:34:54,726 --> 00:34:57,939
Plenty. Taylor's
success just might be due

674
00:34:57,963 --> 00:34:59,874
to insider information.

675
00:34:59,898 --> 00:35:01,642
Oh... Did you talk to SEC?

676
00:35:01,666 --> 00:35:03,911
Well, I tried, but they
gave me the runaround.

677
00:35:03,935 --> 00:35:07,481
It would be a lot easier to
get information if, you know,

678
00:35:07,505 --> 00:35:10,484
if I had an "in" there.

679
00:35:10,508 --> 00:35:13,254
You want me to call
Harry Denton, don't you?

680
00:35:13,278 --> 00:35:14,255
Yes.

681
00:35:14,279 --> 00:35:17,158
Oh, Julie, do I have to?

682
00:35:17,182 --> 00:35:19,527
Harry Denton's your friend.

683
00:35:19,551 --> 00:35:21,596
Well, yes, he is, but
every time I call him,

684
00:35:21,620 --> 00:35:22,897
he invites me to dinner,

685
00:35:22,921 --> 00:35:25,600
and then his wife pairs me
off with some friend of hers

686
00:35:25,624 --> 00:35:26,634
who's recently widowed.

687
00:35:26,658 --> 00:35:30,171
Ben, please? I just need to know

688
00:35:30,195 --> 00:35:33,140
if there's any investigation
going on down there

689
00:35:33,164 --> 00:35:35,443
involving Campbell,
Wagner, Campbell

690
00:35:35,467 --> 00:35:37,612
or Taylor Sinclair.

691
00:35:37,636 --> 00:35:40,948
Okay, I'll call him.

692
00:35:40,972 --> 00:35:45,519
But you have to tell Stan
you're still on the case.

693
00:35:47,545 --> 00:35:49,090
Okay.

694
00:35:49,114 --> 00:35:53,484
And you tell Harry's
wife you're taken.

695
00:36:02,661 --> 00:36:04,472
Julie.

696
00:36:04,496 --> 00:36:06,540
Hi. Come on in.

697
00:36:06,564 --> 00:36:08,509
I've been with the mayor all
day... I just got your message.

698
00:36:08,533 --> 00:36:10,468
Stan, please sit down.

699
00:36:12,003 --> 00:36:14,448
Now, I know I'm
on thin ice here,

700
00:36:14,472 --> 00:36:16,384
but I would like your permission

701
00:36:16,408 --> 00:36:18,386
to search Avery
Campbell's house again.

702
00:36:18,410 --> 00:36:19,587
I don't believe
what I'm hearing.

703
00:36:19,611 --> 00:36:21,389
I know you told
me to drop the case,

704
00:36:21,413 --> 00:36:22,423
- Yes.
- And I tried,

705
00:36:22,447 --> 00:36:24,558
believe me, but I can't.

706
00:36:24,582 --> 00:36:26,560
My instincts just won't let me.

707
00:36:26,584 --> 00:36:27,662
Julie...

708
00:36:27,686 --> 00:36:29,664
What kind of a
prosecutor would I be

709
00:36:29,688 --> 00:36:32,033
if I didn't listen
to my instincts?

710
00:36:32,057 --> 00:36:33,401
I'd be terrible.

711
00:36:33,425 --> 00:36:35,036
I'd do everything by rote.

712
00:36:35,060 --> 00:36:37,972
It would be like throwing
all my cases into a computer.

713
00:36:37,996 --> 00:36:38,996
Okay.

714
00:36:40,298 --> 00:36:41,876
- What?
- I said okay.

715
00:36:41,900 --> 00:36:43,511
Go to Avery Campbell's house.

716
00:36:43,535 --> 00:36:45,446
What the hell,
if it's gonna help

717
00:36:45,470 --> 00:36:48,516
to get this out of your
system, go to the moon.

718
00:36:48,540 --> 00:36:50,184
Just go right away.

719
00:36:50,208 --> 00:36:52,153
I will, tonight, on my own time.

720
00:36:52,177 --> 00:36:54,288
It won't cost the
taxpayers a dime.

721
00:36:54,312 --> 00:36:57,305
I'll leave the
key with Jennifer.

722
00:37:00,318 --> 00:37:02,344
Tell me something.

723
00:37:03,722 --> 00:37:05,900
Just what do you expect
to find there anyway?

724
00:37:05,924 --> 00:37:08,936
All I know is that when
the police were there before,

725
00:37:08,960 --> 00:37:11,439
they were looking at
the scene of a suicide,

726
00:37:11,463 --> 00:37:13,140
not a murder.

727
00:37:13,164 --> 00:37:15,576
Maybe they missed something.

728
00:37:15,600 --> 00:37:19,261
Jule... this <i>is</i> the end of it.

729
00:37:51,936 --> 00:37:54,337
♪♪

730
00:39:34,038 --> 00:39:36,083
- Hello.
- Finally! Where have you been?

731
00:39:36,107 --> 00:39:37,785
I can't come to phone right now,

732
00:39:37,809 --> 00:39:39,753
but if you leave your
name and number

733
00:39:39,777 --> 00:39:41,589
at the beep, I'll
get back to you.

734
00:39:41,613 --> 00:39:44,225
Daggone, I hate these things!

735
00:39:44,249 --> 00:39:46,275
Where are you?!

736
00:40:19,384 --> 00:40:21,546
I knew it.

737
00:40:32,830 --> 00:40:34,662
No.

738
00:41:27,685 --> 00:41:30,018
Oh! Come on!

739
00:41:35,893 --> 00:41:38,294
Whoa, lady, or
I'll break your arm.

740
00:41:40,264 --> 00:41:42,576
How did you know I was here?

741
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:44,244
Stanley Hayden.

742
00:41:48,639 --> 00:41:52,286
Would you rather have
the pair you left here...

743
00:41:52,310 --> 00:41:55,556
the night you murdered
Avery Campbell?

744
00:42:01,619 --> 00:42:04,598
You are going to hear
how Richard Wagner

745
00:42:04,622 --> 00:42:09,470
became aware that Taylor
Sinclair was being investigated

746
00:42:09,494 --> 00:42:13,273
by the Securities and
Exchange Commission.

747
00:42:13,297 --> 00:42:16,043
You are going to
hear how he decided

748
00:42:16,067 --> 00:42:21,315
there was only one way
he could save his company...

749
00:42:21,339 --> 00:42:23,951
By firing Ms. Sinclair.

750
00:42:23,975 --> 00:42:28,255
And you are going to
hear how Taylor Sinclair,

751
00:42:28,279 --> 00:42:32,493
terrified that her career
was about to be ruined,

752
00:42:32,517 --> 00:42:37,231
waited until Mr. Wagner
was alone in his office

753
00:42:37,255 --> 00:42:40,667
and killed him
with a candlestick...

754
00:42:40,691 --> 00:42:43,370
the very candlestick

755
00:42:43,394 --> 00:42:48,108
Avery Campbell had been
holding earlier that evening.

756
00:42:48,132 --> 00:42:51,145
And then she assumed two things:

757
00:42:51,169 --> 00:42:55,916
one, his fingerprints
would be on the candlestick;

758
00:42:55,940 --> 00:43:02,056
and two, since he and Mr. Wagner
had argued earlier that evening,

759
00:43:02,080 --> 00:43:05,812
Avery Campbell would
be blamed for the murder.

760
00:43:07,351 --> 00:43:11,565
I'm sorry to say...
she was right.

761
00:43:11,589 --> 00:43:13,567
But she didn't stop there.

762
00:43:13,591 --> 00:43:15,636
She knew there was
always the chance

763
00:43:15,660 --> 00:43:18,038
that somebody would
discover the truth.

764
00:43:18,062 --> 00:43:20,707
So she did the only
thing she could...

765
00:43:20,731 --> 00:43:23,844
She murdered
Avery Campbell, too.

766
00:43:23,868 --> 00:43:27,147
It must have been
relatively easy.

767
00:43:27,171 --> 00:43:32,419
After all, Avery Campbell
had hired Taylor Sinclair...

768
00:43:32,443 --> 00:43:36,090
he had been one of her
most ardent supporters,

769
00:43:36,114 --> 00:43:40,794
and he was surely one
of her most faithful friends.

770
00:43:40,818 --> 00:43:45,299
The last thing Avery
Campbell must have expected

771
00:43:45,323 --> 00:43:48,102
from his protégé that night

772
00:43:48,126 --> 00:43:51,738
was for her to shoot
him in cold blood.

773
00:43:51,762 --> 00:43:54,475
But that is exactly
what she did.

774
00:43:54,499 --> 00:43:56,877
And she did it with his gun,

775
00:43:56,901 --> 00:43:59,646
one she had no doubt
seen on previous visits

776
00:43:59,670 --> 00:44:01,548
to his home.

777
00:44:01,572 --> 00:44:04,084
And then she went
to his typewriter,

778
00:44:04,108 --> 00:44:08,589
and she calmly typed
out a suicide note,

779
00:44:08,613 --> 00:44:11,458
leading everyone
who read it to believe

780
00:44:11,482 --> 00:44:16,597
that Richard Wagner had
been killed out of anger,

781
00:44:16,621 --> 00:44:19,800
and that Avery Campbell
had killed himself

782
00:44:19,824 --> 00:44:22,336
out of despair.

783
00:44:22,360 --> 00:44:26,240
It was a brilliant plan.

784
00:44:26,264 --> 00:44:28,041
It even took into account

785
00:44:28,065 --> 00:44:33,213
the fact that if Taylor Sinclair
were ever confronted by the SEC,

786
00:44:33,237 --> 00:44:36,617
there were two people that
she could blame for everything:

787
00:44:36,641 --> 00:44:40,854
Richard Wagner
and Avery Campbell,

788
00:44:40,878 --> 00:44:43,056
who were dead, of course.

789
00:44:43,080 --> 00:44:45,879
But that's what made it perfect.

790
00:44:48,186 --> 00:44:51,798
Taylor Sinclair is a very smart,

791
00:44:51,822 --> 00:44:56,103
a very successful, a very
accomplished woman.

792
00:44:56,127 --> 00:44:58,572
We will show that she is also

793
00:44:58,596 --> 00:45:04,763
a very cold, calculating
and efficient killer.

794
00:45:06,270 --> 00:45:09,349
She is a woman
without conscience,

795
00:45:09,373 --> 00:45:13,453
she is a woman
without compassion,

796
00:45:13,477 --> 00:45:18,225
and, as you will soon
see, she deserves...

797
00:45:18,249 --> 00:45:22,578
no compassion... from you.

798
00:45:26,157 --> 00:45:28,149
Thank you.

799
00:45:31,162 --> 00:45:35,242
Due to the lateness of the
hour, this court will be in recess

800
00:45:35,266 --> 00:45:38,202
until 9:00 a.m.
tomorrow morning.

801
00:45:52,450 --> 00:45:55,329
How long have you been here?

802
00:45:55,353 --> 00:45:58,532
Long enough to know that
was a brilliant opening statement.

803
00:45:58,556 --> 00:46:01,001
The jury already loves
you and hates her.

804
00:46:01,025 --> 00:46:03,537
You can't... you can't
ask for more than that.

805
00:46:03,561 --> 00:46:05,138
Are you free for dinner?

806
00:46:05,162 --> 00:46:08,141
Yeah! Italian or Chinese?

807
00:46:08,165 --> 00:46:10,444
American. My place.

808
00:46:10,468 --> 00:46:12,279
Fried chicken?

809
00:46:12,303 --> 00:46:14,314
With all the trimmings.

810
00:46:14,338 --> 00:46:15,897
All right!

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
